#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169} :activity/id #uuid "bc665116-5a76-55aa-9353-754f3071777d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/math-academy-id 6679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87aacf8e-bc15-5baf-95e2-dc84ee4735c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306441} {:answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef304257-c02b-5b9a-91de-da2bbd172314" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cafd04c6-8f34-538e-bfbf-4314bcafa2be" :question/math-academy-id "q-333168" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c91bcfc-99f4-5bfe-804e-7364e7854038"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b7917-2ac5-5b52-a8e9-071c6e798cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "036131be-8602-586a-8ee5-fe7271608cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-332723" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "748c2caa-2098-5b2d-8ce4-acf93b07faac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a3db07d-09b8-53c0-a769-ee5a0527289c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305153} {:answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d58e7d-e179-5799-97c4-0c9c22e0553a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d63971f5-c416-5771-8c44-552fcfc50a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332936" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adfad238-f91c-58f5-8dc5-b475c8a368d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca491ec8-db7c-5b57-b504-cd04e9792609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307030} {:answer/id #uuid "3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{5x}+15xe^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160} :answer-field/id #uuid "416fb058-4b84-52e5-8668-5c9c862047fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceb57bf0-d702-511f-a8e6-4fe4eee1bf79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332938" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c64f98e2-0858-55ea-8d34-04c204a10d14" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420159}]}
